From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] windows.ui.xaml: compiled x:Bind in templates takes its values; TextBox, ScrollViewer, RelativePanel, Popup, ListView

Compiled x:Bind in control templates now takes its values: once a template that is an x:Bind scope is made for a control and its elements are connected, the scope's binding connector is connected to its data root, the control (Connect with the template's own ID) -- the binding object then registers for the control's members and sets its elements, as observed with NanaZip's compiled templates (its status bar shows its counts). The address bar's template needed objects we did not have, and its bindings ended in an access violation without them: TextBox (Text, MaxLength, read-only, alignment, wrapping, placeholder and header members, TextChanged, Select/SelectAll; its text shown in its template's ContentElement, or in the desktop's own field when it has no template; keyboard editing comes next), ScrollViewer (a content control; scroll settings kept, attached ones too), RelativePanel (panel alignments; relations between children not yet), Popup (its child kept out of the layout; not shown yet), ItemsControl and ListView (ItemsSource, Items, templates kept; not shown yet). Compiled enum values ("#enumN") convert for any enum member; more enums known by name (ScrollMode, ScrollBarVisibility, ZoomMode, TextTrimming, Stretch, BackgroundSizing, PenLineCap/Join). Interface identity per windows-rs 0.30.0 (MIT); the implementation is ours. Behind the XAML switch of 6131.
---
